---
import {
  eazeModernApproach as modernApproach,
  eazeRealTimeComplexity as realTimeComplexity,
} from "@/data/eazegames-case-study";
---

<section class="wrap region eaze-reading stack" aria-labelledby="realtime-title">
  <p class="kick"><span class="idx">05</span> Real-time product complexity</p>
  <h2 id="realtime-title" class="section-title">Live state had to remain legible, not merely fast.</h2>
  <ul class="eaze-list eaze-list-wide">
    {realTimeComplexity.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="wrap region eaze-split" aria-label="Foundation and delivery">
  <div class="stack">
    <p class="kick"><span class="idx">06</span> Reusable foundation</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Shared conventions before “design system” became the default label.</h2>
    <p>
      I created a reusable component and utility library that became the frontend foundation as the product and team
      expanded. This reduced repeated implementation work and provided shared interaction and styling conventions across
      authenticated flows.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="stack">
    <p class="kick"><span class="idx">07</span> Delivery & team growth</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">From two people to a six-person team and production delivery.</h2>
    <p>
      I joined the initial two-person team. After the prototype proved successful, the project budget and team expanded
      to six. I set up frontend CI, participated in client planning and estimation, reviewed code, interviewed
      candidates, and mentored junior developers through production delivery.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap region stack" aria-labelledby="outcome-title">
  <p class="kick"><span class="idx">08</span> Outcome & durability</p>
  <h2 id="outcome-title" class="section-title">Supported outcomes, without borrowing today’s metrics.</h2>
  <div class="eaze-outcomes">
    <p><strong>~1.5 years</strong><span>Original frontend delivered to production</span></p>
    <p><strong>2 → 6</strong><span>Team growth following prototype success</span></p>
    <p><strong>Version 1</strong><span>Shared UI and utility foundation for the growing codebase</span></p>
    <p><strong>Active today</strong><span>Independently evolved product, maintained by EazeGames</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap region eaze-retrospective" aria-labelledby="retrospective-title">
  <div class="stack">
    <p class="kick"><span class="idx">09</span> Historical retrospective</p>
    <h2 id="retrospective-title" class="section-title">What the Webpack 2 era taught me.</h2>
    <p>
      This project predates today’s mature React frameworks and typed full-stack contracts. It taught me how to
      structure a large client application, isolate integration boundaries, create reusable UI foundations, and manage
      real-time product state while a product and team were both growing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel stack">
    <h3 class="panel-title">How I would approach it today</h3>
    <ul class="eaze-list">
      {modernApproach.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<aside class="wrap region eaze-continuity stack" aria-labelledby="continuity-title">
  <p class="kick"><span class="idx">LIVE</span> Product continuity</p>
  <h2 id="continuity-title" class="section-title">Current product context, clearly separated from my work.</h2>
  <p class="measure">
    EazeGames remains active today. Its current team has substantially redesigned and further developed the product
    since my engagement. Visit the current product for context; the live interface and current implementation are not
    presented as my work.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="btn" data-kind="ghost" href="https://eazegames.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      Current product, maintained by EazeGames ↗
    </a>
  </p>
  <p class="eaze-disclaimer">
    I designed the frontend architecture and implemented the original web application during my 2016-2017 engagement.
    EazeGames remains an active product and has since been redesigned and further developed by its current team. The
    archival screenshots above document the Version 1 interface I worked on; the live site is linked only for current
    product context.
  </p>
</aside>
